<header><p class="st-kicker">other · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Muskogee</h1><p class="st-meta">1799 CE – 1803 CE · capital Miccosukee</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-proclaimed Creek and Seminole state in Spanish Florida led by the adventurer William Augustus Bowles, which challenged Spain for four years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-augustus-bowles">William Augustus Bowles</a>, a Maryland-born Loyalist who had married into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/muscogee-confederacy">Muscogee Confederacy</a>, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1799 after escaping Spanish captivity. Backed by British trading interests and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seminole-nation">Seminole</a> leaders, he proclaimed the State of Muskogee, with its seat at the Miccosukee towns near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallahassee">Tallahassee</a>, and declared himself its director general.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Muskogee was less a working state than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-augustus-bowles">Bowles</a>'s project for an independent Native nation allied to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. He issued proclamations, granted commissions to privateers and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1800, briefly capturing the Spanish fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-san-marcos-de-apalache">San Marcos de Apalache</a>. He tried to break the trading monopoly of the firm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panton-leslie-and-company">Panton, Leslie and Company</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> offered a reward for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-augustus-bowles">Bowles</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> leaders allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> turned against him. In 1803 he was seized at a council at Hickory Ground and handed to the Spanish. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morro-castle-havana">Morro Castle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> in 1805, and the state vanished with him.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-muskogee.jpg" alt="State of Muskogee" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: William Augustus Bowles in 1799 [2] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_of_Muskogee_(Florida,_1799-1803).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1799 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Miccosukee</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Augustus Bowles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muscogee, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muskogee State</dd></dl></aside></div>

